n-names></name><name><surname>Cao</surname><given-names>YX</given-names></name><name><surname>Xu</surname><given-names>CB</given-names></name><name><surname>Edvinsson</surname><given-names>L</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Lipid-soluble smoke particles damage endothelial cells and reduce endothelium-dependent dilatation in rat and man</article-title><source>BMC Cardiovasc Disord</source><year>2006</year><volume>6</volume><fpage>3</fpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16423279</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b32-vhrm-5-333"><label>32.</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Saavedra</surname><given-names>JM</given-names></name><name><surname>Benicky</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Zhou</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Mechanisms of the anti-ischemic effect of angiotensin II AT( 1 ) receptor antagonists in the brain</article-title><source>Cell Mol Neurobiol</source><year>2006</year><volume>26</volume><fpage>1099</fpage><lpage>1111</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16636899</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b33-vhrm-5-333"><label>33.</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Edvinsson</surname><given-names>L</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Cerebrovascular angiotensin AT1 receptor regulation in cerebral ischemia</article-title><source>Trends Cardiovasc Med</source><year>2008</year><volume>18</volume><fpage>98</fpage><lpage>103</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">18436148</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b34-vhrm-5-333"><label>34.</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Ando</surname><given-names>H</given-names></name><name><surname>Zhou</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Macova</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Imboden</surname><given-names>H</given-names></name><name><surname>Saavedra</surname><given-names>JM</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Angiotensin II AT1 receptor blockade reverses pathological hypertrophy and inflammation in brain microvessels of spontaneously hypertensive rats</article-title><source>Stroke</source><year>2004</year><volume>35</volume><fpage>1726</fpage><lpage>1731</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15143297</pub-id></citation></ref></ref-list></back><floats-wrap><fig id="f1-vhrm-5-333" position="float"><label>Figure 1</label><caption><p>The expression of the inflammatory genes tended to be greater in the cerebral arteries treated with DSP than in the cerebral arteries treated with DMSO control. There was a significant upregulation of the extracellular-matrix-related genes (MMP9 and MMP13) in the DSP group, compared with DMSO control. The cerebral arteries have been organ cultured for 24 hours in the presence of DSP or DMSO (control).</p><p><bold>Notes:</bold> Data presented as mean &#x000b1; SEM, n = 4; *p &#x0003c; 0.05.</p><p><bold>Abbreviations:</bold> DMSO, dimethyl sulfoxide; DSP, cigarette smoke particles; MMP, matrix metalloproteinase; ns, not significant.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="vhrm-5-333f1"/></fig><fig id="f2-vhrm-5-333" position="float"><label>Figure 2</label><caption><p>The expression of angiotensin II receptors in the cerebral arteries following organ culture with DSP or DMSO (control), there was a significant upregulation of the AT<sub>1</sub> receptors induced by DSP compared with DMSO.</p><p><bold>Notes:</bold> Data presented as mean &#x000b1; SEM, n = 4; *p &#x0003c; 0.05.</p><p><bold>Abbreviations:</bold> AT, angiotensin receptor; DMSO, dimethyl sulfoxide; DSP, cigarette smoke particles; ns, not significant.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="vhrm-5-333f2"/></fig><fig id="f3-vhrm-5-333" position="float"><label>Figure 3</label><caption><p>No p38 phosphorylation was found in the fresh cerebral arteries, while a slight increase was obtained in the cerebral arteries treated with DMSO and a strong activation in the cerebral arteries treated with DSP, ATF-2 and Elk-1 showed the same pattern as the p38 phosphorylation. However, phosphorylation of Elk-1 was also seen in the fresh cerebral arteries.</p><p><bold>Abbreviations:</bold> DMSO, dimethyl sulfoxide; DSP, cigarette smoke particles.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="vhrm-5-333f3"/></fig><fig id="f4-vhrm-5-333" position="float"><label>Figure 4</label><caption><p>Immunohistochemistry shows a significant increase in the amount of phosphorylated MMP13 proteins and AT<sub>1</sub> receptor proteins in the cerebral arteries treated with DSP, compared with the control (DMSO-treated cerebral arteries). However, there was no increase in AT<sub>2</sub> receptor proteins induced by DSP.</p><p><bold>Notes:</bold> The size marker represents 20 &#x003bc;m.</p><p><bold>Abbreviations:</bold> AT, angiotensin receptor; DMSO, dimethyl sulfoxide; DSP, cigarette smoke particles; MMP, matrix metalloproteinase.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="vhrm-5-333f4"/></fig><table-wrap id="t1-vhrm-5-333" position="float"><label>Table 1</label><caption><p>Gene-specific primer sequences and accession numbers</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><bold>Gene name</bold></th><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><bold>Abbreviation</bold></th><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><bold>Accession number</bold></th><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><bold>Primer sequence</bold></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Beta-actin</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x003b2;-actin</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">NM-031144</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">gtagccatccaggctgtgttg<break/>tgccagtggtacgaccagag</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Elongating factor 1</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">EF-1</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">NM-175838</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">gcaagcccatgtgtgttgaa<break/>tgatgacacccacagcaactg</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Interleukin 6</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">IL6</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">NM-012589</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">aagagacttccagccagttgcc<break/>actggtctgttgtgggtggtatc</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Inducible no synthase</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">iNOS</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">NM-012611</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">caatggcttgaggcagaagc<break/>gccacctcggatatctcttgc</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Matrix metalloproteinase 9</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">MMP9</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">NM-031055</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">aagccttggtgtggcacgac<break/>tggaaatacgcagggtttgc</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Matrix metalloproteinase 13</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">MMP13</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">M60616</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">tctgcaccctcagcaggttg<break/>catgaggtctcgggatggatg</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Angiotensin II receptor 1</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">AT<sub>1</sub></td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">NM-030985</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">ggtggagtgacaggttcaaagc<break/>agggtgaatggtcctttggtc</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Angiotensin II receptor 2</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">AT<sub>2</sub></td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">U01908</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">tctgttagtgggatgcatgtaatca<break/>tgtgggcctccaaaccatt</td></tr></tbody></table></table-wrap><table-wrap id="t2-vhrm-5-333" position="float"><label>Table 2</label><caption><p>Phosphorylation of intracellular signal proteins induced by DSP</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><bold>Fresh</bold></th><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><bold>DMSO-treated</bold></th><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><bold>DSP-treated</bold></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">p-p38</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1 &#x000b1; 0.26</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1.15 &#x000b1; 0.38</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1.81 &#x000b1; 0.26<xref ref-type="table-fn" rid="tfn2-vhrm-5-333">*</xref></td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">p-ERK 1/2</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1 &#x000b1; 0.18</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1.58 &#x000b1; 0.13</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1.83 &#x000b1; 0.40</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">p-SAPK/JNK</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1 &#x000b1; 0.03</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1.51 &#x000b1; 0.34</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1.79 &#x000b1; 0.09</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">p-ATF-2</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1 &#x000b1; 0.12</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1.14 &#x000b1; 0.09</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1.61 &#x000b1; 0.37<xref ref-type="table-fn" rid="tfn2-vhrm-5-333">*</xref></td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">p-Elk-1</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1 &#x000b1; 0.19</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1.08 &#x000b1; 0.54</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">2.18 &#x000b1; 0.09<xref ref-type="table-fn" rid="tfn2-vhrm-5-333">*</xref></td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">p-c-Jun</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1 &#x000b1; 0.49</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1.81 &#x000b1; 0.25</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1.88 &#x000b1; 0.27</td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot><fn id="tfn1-vhrm-5-333"><p><bold>Notes:</bold> Data presented as mean &#x000b1; SEM, n = 4;</p></fn><fn id="tfn2-vhrm-5-333"><label>*</label><p>p &#x0003c; 0.05 when comparing DSP with DMSO control.</p></fn><fn id="tfn3-vhrm-5-333"><p><bold>Abbreviations:</bold> DMSO, dimethyl sulfoxide; DSP, cigarette smoke particles.</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap></floats-wrap></article>